    "schema_version": "lolla.reasoning_process_view_specific_input.v1",
    "source": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tMy friend Jonah and I have spent eighteen months making an audio documentary.\ne002\tWe were invited to premiere it at a respected local showcase in six weeks.\ne003\tI produced the reporting and interviews; he built the sound and structure.\ne004\tAfter listening to the latest cut, I think the final third needs a major re-edit.\ne005\tJonah thinks we should finish what we have and learn from an audience.\ne006\tWe are also avoiding a conversation about whether the credit should simply say “by both of us” or name our roles.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne007\tThe edit and credit issues are related because both ask how your different contributions translate into authority.\ne008\tSix weeks may be enough for targeted revision but not for rebuilding the final third without sacrificing the rest.\ne009\tBefore deciding, identify what problem you hear: factual confusion, emotional shape, pacing, or discomfort that the piece is becoming final.\ne010\tOn credit, naming roles can recognize distinct work, but it should not become a substitute for agreeing who decides what.\n[Turn 2 USER]\ne011\tI do not think this is fear of finishing.\ne012\tThe ending makes our main participant sound more certain about leaving her town than she was.\ne013\tWe did not fabricate anything, but Jonah used her strongest lines together and removed hesitation.\ne014\tHe says the whole piece compresses time and that the uncertainty is clear earlier.\ne015\tI feel responsible because I conducted the interviews.\ne016\tThis is not just my taste about pacing.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne017\tThat changes the priority.\ne018\tIf the edit alters how a participant's position is understood, you need to review it against the full interview and the promises you made her, not only against dramatic effect.\ne019\tYou and Jonah may still disagree about how much hesitation belongs in the ending, but the disagreement should be grounded in representation.\ne020\tA focused re-edit of that thread could be necessary even if the larger structure remains.\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne021\tYou moved very quickly to my side.\ne022\tJonah has listened to the full interviews too, and the participant approved a cut three months ago.\ne023\tThe current ending is not identical, but it uses the same recorded material.\ne024\tShe also told us she did not want to supervise every edit.\ne025\tIf I contact her whenever I am uncomfortable, I could be shifting authorship onto her and making Jonah feel I use ethics as a veto.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne026\tThat is fair.\ne027\tApproval and her wish not to manage the edit are real constraints.\ne028\tContacting her is not automatically the respectful option.\ne029\tYou could first compare the approved cut and current ending yourselves, describe the interpretive change without arguing motives, and decide whether it crosses the scope of what she approved.\ne030\tIf you cannot agree, a limited factual or consent question may be better than asking her to adjudicate your artistic conflict.\n[Turn 4 USER]\ne031\tThere is history here.\ne032\tEarly on we said major structural decisions would be mutual.\ne033\tIn practice, Jonah made many of them because he was in the session every day, and I usually trusted him.\ne034\tNow he says I am reopening the structure at the last moment after being less available.\ne035\tThat is partly true.\ne036\tI was reporting another project for two months.\ne037\tBut I also think “you were not in the room” can turn into permanent final say.\n[Turn 4 ASSISTANT]\ne038\tThe informal agreement and the working reality diverged.\ne039\tJonah accumulated practical authorship through sustained editing, while you did not explicitly surrender mutual authority.\ne040\tBoth facts deserve recognition.\ne041\tA fair process now may give greater weight to his knowledge of the cut without treating that as unilateral ownership.\ne042\tThe harder question is what mutual decision means under a deadline: consensus on every change, a defined area of final say, or a rule for what happens when you remain split.\n[Turn 5 USER]\ne043\tA curator from the showcase listened yesterday.\ne044\tShe loved the ending and said it was the first time the piece felt inevitable.\ne045\tThat word bothered me, but it also means Jonah achieved something.\ne046\tShe can move us to a later program, though she cannot guarantee the same room or promotion.\ne047\tJonah thinks postponing would throw away momentum for a concern the participant may not share.\ne048\tI am starting to wonder whether I am asking the piece to preserve every ambiguity because I am afraid of making an argument.\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne049\tDocumentaries do make arguments through selection, and preserving every hesitation can be another form of distortion.\ne050\tThe curator's response is useful evidence about the ending's force, not evidence that its interpretation is fair.\ne051\tThe later program reduces the deadline's finality but introduces real opportunity costs.\ne052\tYou might distinguish changes required for representational integrity from changes that would make the argument feel more like yours.\ne053\tThat distinction will not be perfectly objective, but trying to articulate it may clarify the disagreement.\n[Turn 6 USER]\ne054\tJonah proposed restoring one moment of hesitation and adding a short scene earlier that shows the participant's obligations in the town.\ne055\tHe would keep the final sequence.\ne056\tI can imagine that working.\ne057\tBut he also wants the credit to read “A film by Jonah Vale and Mira Chen,” even though it is audio, because the showcase uses that language.\ne058\tI want “Reported by Mira Chen, sound and story by Jonah Vale.”\ne059\tHe hears my version as splitting the work into journalism and art.\n[Turn 6 ASSISTANT]\ne060\tHis edit proposal sounds like a substantive attempt to preserve uncertainty without dissolving the ending, though you should judge it in the cut rather than in description.\ne061\tThe credit disagreement is not merely wording; each version tells a story about what the work is.\ne062\tEqual top-line authorship plus a second line naming reporting, editing, and sound may acknowledge collaboration and labor, but only if neither of you experiences the top line as erasing the actual imbalance you are trying to name.\n[Turn 7 USER]\ne063\tMy provisional plan is to hear Jonah's revised cut before deciding about postponement.\ne064\tI will compare it with the approved version and write down the exact interpretive difference I still see, if any.\ne065\tFor credit, I will propose equal top-line authorship with detailed role credits below, but I am not sure I can accept that if he still treats structural decisions as his domain.\ne066\tI want to separate the current film from the rules for our next collaboration, and I am not sure they can really be separated.\n[Turn 7 ASSISTANT]\ne067\tListening before deciding prevents an abstract disagreement from hardening around a cut that does not yet exist.\ne068\tYour uncertainty about credit and future authority is also legitimate: a current credit can acknowledge completed work, while the feelings behind it may still determine whether you collaborate again.\ne069\tYou do not need to solve the next project before the showcase, but you may need to avoid using a harmonious credit line as evidence that the underlying process felt mutual.\ne070\tThe revised cut could resolve the edit question and leave the partnership question open.",
